.TH HD-NETWORK-IFACE 8 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-network-iface \- add, change and remove interfaces in the hyprdebian network settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-network-iface list
.br
.B hd-network-iface show
.I name
.br
.B hd-network-iface set
.I name
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hd-network-iface remove
.I name
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-network-iface
manages the wired and wifi interfaces in the key
.B hd-network
in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json .
See
.BR hd-network (7)
for the settings.
.PP
It reads all the network settings, makes the change, checks the result the
same way
.BR hd-network-render (8)
does, and writes it with
.BR hd-config-update (1).
When the check fails, nothing is written.
When no settings are left, the key is removed with
.BR hd-config-remove (1).
.PP
It does not change the network itself: run
.B hd-network-render
afterwards.
.PP
.B set
and
.B remove
are run as root.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List the interfaces, one per line, with a summary of their settings.
.TP
.BI show " name"
Show the settings of an interface as JSON.
.TP
.BI set " name"
Add an interface, or change its settings.
Settings that are not given stay as they are; a new interface without
options uses DHCP.
.I name
is an interface name, such as
.BR enp3s0 ,
or a glob, such as
.BR en* ;
quote a glob so the shell does not expand it.
.TP
.BI remove " name"
Remove an interface from the settings.
.SH OPTIONS
These options are for
.BR set .
.TP
.BI \-\-bridge " bridge"
Make the interface a port of
.IR bridge ,
which must be set up with
.BR hd-network-bridge (8)
first.
A port has no addresses, so this drops the other settings of the interface,
and cannot be combined with the options below.
A wifi interface cannot be a port.
.TP
.B \-\-dhcp
Get addresses with DHCPv4, IPv6 router advertisements and DHCPv6.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dhcp
Do not use DHCP or router advertisements.
.TP
.BI \-\-address " address/prefix"
A static address, such as
.BR 192.168.1.10/24 .
May be given more than once; replaces the addresses that were set.
.TP
.BI \-\-gateway " address"
The default gateway.
May be given more than once, for IPv4 and IPv6; replaces the gateways that
were set.
.TP
.BI \-\-dns " address"
A DNS server.
May be given more than once; replaces the DNS servers that were set.
.TP
.BI \-\-metric " n"
The metric of the routes; the default is 100.
.TP
.BI \-\-unset " setting"
Return a setting to its default:
.BR addresses ,
.BR bridge ,
.BR dhcp ,
.BR dns ,
.B gateway
or
.BR metric .
Applied before the other options, so
.B \-\-unset bridge
followed by address options turns a port into an interface with addresses
of its own.
May be given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the command succeeded, 1 on an error, 2 on wrong arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
The configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
Use DHCP on every ethernet interface, and on the wifi interface with a
higher metric:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-network-iface set 'en*'
hd-network-iface set wlp2s0 \-\-metric 200
.fi
.RE
.PP
Give enp4s0 a static address:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-network-iface set enp4s0 \-\-no\-dhcp \-\-address 192.168.1.10/24 \e
    \-\-gateway 192.168.1.1 \-\-dns 192.168.1.1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Make enp3s0 a port of br0, and later give it DHCP again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-network-iface set enp3s0 \-\-bridge br0
hd-network-iface set enp3s0 \-\-unset bridge
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply the changes:
.PP
.RS
hd-network-render
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-network (7),
.BR hd-network-bridge (8),
.BR hd-network-render (8),
.BR hd-config-update (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Erik Van Hamme <erik.vanhamme@gmail.com>
